WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.622
CraftingError,

00:00:02.622 --> 00:00:05.548
(irrational polygenesis)

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.982
I’ve been studying and practicing for what seems like a long time. I was

00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:15.854
formally trained, I’ve read about the history of the work in my field, and I’ve

00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:20.788
traveled to other countries to study how other people practice. I’ve paid fairly

00:00:20.788 --> 00:00:25.659
close attention to what’s going on in the various professional communities that

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:30.593
border my field, as well as how markets and media respond to this work. I have a

00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:34.840
reasonable summary handle on the historical scholarship, contemporary

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.586
practices, and even some of the critical theory that people have developed to

00:00:39.586 --> 00:00:44.645
explain some of the phenomenon and links between the field and the larger culture.

00:00:44.645 --> 00:00:49.391
But, even armed with these filters, I haven’t quite pinned down the nature of

00:00:49.391 --> 00:00:50.016
this work.

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:56.375
“Each generation that discovers something from its experience must pass that on,

00:00:56.375 --> 00:01:01.851
but it must pass that on with a delicate balance of respect and disrespect, so

00:01:01.851 --> 00:01:07.470
that the race—now that it is aware of the disease to which it is liable—does not

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.235
inflict its errors too rigidly on its youth, but it does pass on the

00:01:12.235 --> 00:01:16.716
accumulated wisdom, plus the wisdom that it may not be wisdom.”.

00:01:16.716 --> 00:01:23.757
Footnote 2. Richard P Feynman, quoted from The Pleasure of Finding Things Out

00:01:23.757 --> 00:01:30.891
(the best short works of RPF,) Perseus Books, 1999, pages 171-188. In 1966 RPF

00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:37.839
gave a lecture to the National Science Teachers Association in New York City

00:01:37.839 --> 00:01:44.880
called What is Science? He talked about how the human race learns, explaining

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:52.292
that individuals acquire new experiences about as fast as we forget about others.

00:01:52.292 --> 00:01:53.867
We accumulate va.

00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:58.555
Sometimes my work is about the elimination of error. A positivist spin

00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:03.446
may be that my work is about the creation and maximization of beauty, and

00:02:03.446 --> 00:02:08.813
recognizing error is how I evaluate my progress toward that goal. These might be

00:02:08.813 --> 00:02:13.908
errors in idea, in approach, in form, in color, in surface, in presentation,

00:02:13.908 --> 00:02:19.004
performance, or longevity. My primary tool to work through these provisional

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:24.371
errors is the pencil. I’m an artist, I sketch through as many of my bad ideas as

00:02:24.371 --> 00:02:29.330
possible before I waste time in slower, more dimensional materials. I talk

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.629
through my ideas with other people and give my students feedback on their ideas

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:39.656
because I think it will help us to discard the ugly, useless, and stupid—as

00:02:39.656 --> 00:02:45.159
well as point out the opportunities we’ve so far overlooked. But over the past few

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:50.322
years I’ve been losing some of my faith in that process. Not just because the

00:02:50.322 --> 00:02:55.554
trends in art and design have rewarded the loose and intuitive, but because as

00:02:55.554 --> 00:03:00.921
I’ve gained more experience, I’ve gotten better at seeing flaws in things I once

00:03:00.921 --> 00:03:05.404
thought perfect. As a young artist I thought that once my skills of

00:03:05.404 --> 00:03:10.636
observation and making improved, the quality of my work would improve. But the

00:03:10.636 --> 00:03:14.440
number of facets and flaws I can see have also increased.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:20.019
What if errors aren’t avoidable? What if we can avoid them only by squinting or

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:25.741
looking away from them? What if they’re necessary? What if everything, (if looked

00:03:25.741 --> 00:03:30.819
at from particular formal, conceptual, material, ecological, functional,

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:34.967
referential, or narrative points of view) is ALWAYS fallen?

00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:40.110
Learning to do something the right way implies there is a wrong way. Sometimes

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:45.321
those wrong ways (perhaps more numerous than the right) are a large part of the

00:03:45.321 --> 00:03:49.863
educational process in a new craft. Whether or not there was a stated

00:03:49.863 --> 00:03:54.739
injunction, once we see a defect, we point it out, we can talk about it, a

00:03:54.739 --> 00:04:00.083
decision can be made as to whether or not it is in need of correction, whether it

00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:04.825
is profitable to correct it and what various consequences of the revised

00:04:04.825 --> 00:04:09.701
widget might be. This is a skill. One of my favorite practical books as an

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:14.978
undergraduate was Web Pages that Suck. Not just because it was entertaining, but

00:04:14.978 --> 00:04:20.055
because it was a rare critical idea at the time: showing and pointing out the

00:04:20.055 --> 00:04:25.332
bad in order to instruct us toward the good. Recognizing the relative quality of

00:04:25.332 --> 00:04:30.275
inputs and their manipulation is an important critical faculty — whether or

00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:34.550
not the project suggests that we should minimize or eliminate it.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.569
What is it that I do, and why do I do it?

00:04:37.569 --> 00:04:39.427
“I craft dreams.”.

00:04:39.427 --> 00:04:41.795
“Because it’s what I am good at.”.

00:04:41.795 --> 00:04:46.811
“I make things that have never existed, that no one yet needs.”.

00:04:46.811 --> 00:04:50.108
“Because I took a co-op class in high school.”.

00:04:50.108 --> 00:04:52.894
“I teach students to be CRITICALLY creative.”.

00:04:52.894 --> 00:04:54.612
“Because I had a great teacher.”.

00:04:54.612 --> 00:04:56.237
“I make jewelry.”.

00:04:56.237 --> 00:04:57.770
“Because of Calder.”.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.860
“I surprise people.”.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.228
“Because I like detail.”.

00:05:02.228 --> 00:05:04.596
“I make sense of the world.”.

00:05:04.596 --> 00:05:06.407
“Because I need the money.”.

00:05:06.407 --> 00:05:08.868
“Because someone inspired me.”.

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:11.283
“I try to prove that I am smart.”.

00:05:11.283 --> 00:05:14.627
“Because I like working with my hands.”.

00:05:14.627 --> 00:05:19.085
“I pay attention to beautiful and surprising things.”.

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:22.011
“Because there is so much to learn about.”.

00:05:22.011 --> 00:05:26.469
“I juxtapose incongruous elements from source material.”.

00:05:26.469 --> 00:05:29.070
“Because of Treasures of Tutankhamun.”.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.671
“I make things to be desired.”.

00:05:31.671 --> 00:05:34.690
“Because my work might survive me.”.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:40.419
The overall process that I tell people I follow, that I teach students to follow,

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:40.634
is:

00:05:40.634 --> 00:05:43.420
Research, Brainstorm, Ideate,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.021
Design, Revise, Render,

00:05:46.021 --> 00:05:49.318
Present, (Repeat ∞).

00:05:49.318 --> 00:05:54.680
I identify formal problems and vacancies and try to solve and take advantage of

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.768
them with new work. I look into a subject, try to find opportunities or new

00:05:59.768 --> 00:06:05.405
ideas based on that subject, go through a number of variations in quick succession,

00:06:05.405 --> 00:06:10.836
give something a provisional form, revise it, execute it in an appropriate fixed

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:16.199
form and then document and present it to some audience — sometimes for feedback

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:21.286
that will jump the process back to a previous point, or force me to abandon

00:06:21.286 --> 00:06:25.480
further iterations. Research, brainstorming, ideation, design,

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:30.567
revision, rendering, presentation. Everything is recursive; I make lists of

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:35.586
the things I want to learn and then lists of the things I need to learn to

00:06:35.586 --> 00:06:40.880
understand those things and then lists of things I need to learn to understand

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:41.842
those things….

00:06:41.842 --> 00:06:46.779
I make and consume non-verbal things for pretty much the same reasons; adventuring

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:51.534
through and being surprised by little adjustments in ways of looking at things.

00:06:51.534 --> 00:06:56.471
I’m not making breakthroughs. I am trying to learn about the world I’m making work

00:06:56.471 --> 00:07:00.372
in through the products others design and the objects I’m making.

00:07:00.372 --> 00:07:06.593
The seven steps in this process, when given two weeks for each, are perfect for

00:07:06.593 --> 00:07:12.894
a fifteen week college course. Research: investigate, read. Brainstorm: generate

00:07:12.894 --> 00:07:19.115
ideas, evaluate. Ideation: develop the best of them, discriminate. Design: give

00:07:19.115 --> 00:07:25.257
them shape, fit them into the world. Revise: interrogate, change. Render: give

00:07:25.257 --> 00:07:30.521
them form, presence. Present: tell everyone how you got there, take

00:07:30.521 --> 00:07:36.184
questions. But that process and that timing is both a contraction and an

00:07:36.184 --> 00:07:42.166
expansion of non academic workflows. It’s is a contraction because, for very

00:07:42.166 --> 00:07:47.829
complicated projects or for certain individuals, there will be many more

00:07:47.829 --> 00:07:53.571
steps (some of which will be substeps in a category related to those I’ve

00:07:53.571 --> 00:07:54.289
listed.).

00:07:54.289 --> 00:07:59.644
Consider the design and construction of a skyscraper: there may be a small, initial

00:07:59.644 --> 00:08:04.607
team involved in conceptual research, brainstorming, ideation, and design—and

00:08:04.607 --> 00:08:09.635
certainly they will do a lot of rendering and presentation to win the call for

00:08:09.635 --> 00:08:14.206
proposals—and they will often be specialists with particular expertise.

00:08:14.206 --> 00:08:19.300
So, the process would need to expand in order to meet the needs of the project.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:24.001
The research, design, revision, and rendering stages could very easily be

00:08:24.001 --> 00:08:28.964
meaningfully subdivided and augmented with additional stages, each with their

00:08:28.964 --> 00:08:33.731
own team of different specialists. Research, for instance, might be broken

00:08:33.731 --> 00:08:38.825
out into components that deal with the site, materials and manufacturing, usage

00:08:38.825 --> 00:08:43.853
and programming, visual design of the structure, energy use and physical plant

00:08:43.853 --> 00:08:48.751
issues, and regulatory issues. While there is an implied reciprocity between

00:08:48.751 --> 00:08:53.910
design and revision that could logically include formal engineering concerns—for

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:58.416
something like a skyscraper there could easily be more engineering and

00:08:58.416 --> 00:09:03.770
construction substages than there is work in any other stage—and they could be more

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:08.929
expensive and persistent. Another implied stage in the design process that would

00:09:08.929 --> 00:09:14.023
likely be given insufficient expression using only these seven categories would

00:09:14.023 --> 00:09:18.986
be required approvals. While there are multiple points within the design of a

00:09:18.986 --> 00:09:23.753
shoe to have a manager sign off, the consequences of having to repeat work

00:09:23.753 --> 00:09:28.846
that hasn’t been signed off on would be relatively minor. For a large building,

00:09:28.846 --> 00:09:31.393
the consequences could be CATASTROPHIC.

00:09:31.393 --> 00:09:36.439
Seven stages are also an expansion of real workflows; for many people and for

00:09:36.439 --> 00:09:41.353
smaller projects, there is little mental or formal separation between these

00:09:41.353 --> 00:09:46.532
stages. The steps of brainstorming and ideation seem especially artificial if I

00:09:46.532 --> 00:09:51.777
consider the way an oil painting might come into being. There are artists who do

00:09:51.777 --> 00:09:56.425
exhaustive studies that might be considered separately as brainstorming

00:09:56.425 --> 00:10:01.139
when they are loose and then be called ideation as they approach what is

00:10:01.139 --> 00:10:06.318
eventually cartooned onto the canvas. But, to the extent that most artists do a

00:10:06.318 --> 00:10:11.497
lot of preparatory work in ideation, they are either doing it in their heads or

00:10:11.497 --> 00:10:16.278
through repetition in their primary materials. Revision is something more

00:10:16.278 --> 00:10:20.793
organic for some of us, (with small, non-articulated changes based on

00:10:20.793 --> 00:10:26.038
instinct, experience and feedback.) While someone building a one of a kind chair

00:10:26.038 --> 00:10:31.018
might tweak some detail as they are shaving the spokes, it’s far more likely

00:10:31.018 --> 00:10:36.330
that some details will be revised in the next chair. There is a lot of individual

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:41.442
and circumstantial variability to the stages of developing an idea and thing —

00:10:41.442 --> 00:10:46.555
but it’s reasonable to assume that the smaller the project (in scale, in time,

00:10:46.555 --> 00:10:51.668
in resources needed to develop it,) and the more experienced the developer (in

00:10:51.668 --> 00:10:56.780
practice, through accumulated standards, in well chosen constraints,) the more

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.768
organic and indivisible these stages might be.

00:10:59.768 --> 00:11:04.264
A scaffolded process makes us feel like we are in control, that we are

00:11:04.264 --> 00:11:08.239
approaching problems systematically—breaking them into smaller

00:11:08.239 --> 00:11:12.735
pieces, combining approaches to each and reaching reasonable solutions

00:11:12.735 --> 00:11:16.254
algebraically. But the clockwork isn’t always the same.

00:11:16.254 --> 00:11:21.816
I don’t follow that recipe precisely. The stages in the design process I teach have

00:11:21.816 --> 00:11:27.243
been borrowed from other people who have articulated their process, but my actual

00:11:27.243 --> 00:11:32.670
work product does not go through each in that order and with equal intensity. Few

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:37.893
people probably go through any process, over their lifetime, applying the same

00:11:37.893 --> 00:11:39.521
steps in the same order.

00:11:39.521 --> 00:11:44.740
Let’s imagine that I’ve decided to work on a new widget. The first deviation from

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:49.698
the process comes at the first step—I likely started my research years before

00:11:49.698 --> 00:11:54.787
the idea came up to make this widget. I read, and watch, and do things that can

00:11:54.787 --> 00:12:00.005
(tangentially, at least) inform the work I tend to be involved in making. So, the

00:12:00.005 --> 00:12:04.964
moment the project idea comes to me, I already have a few books of pop social

00:12:04.964 --> 00:12:09.856
science and historical fiction, a few thousand photographs of great and crap

00:12:09.856 --> 00:12:14.880
benchmarks, video and more photos of phenomenological references that might be

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.577
able to be brought into the widget, memories of cracked objects and metal

00:12:19.577 --> 00:12:24.274
buildings and brick streets and tree roots from local and exotic locales.

00:12:24.274 --> 00:12:29.101
These will need to be augmented with particular research, of course. So the

00:12:29.101 --> 00:12:34.255
category of research still exists for me to go through, but it doesn’t exist for

00:12:34.255 --> 00:12:38.691
me the way that it exists for my students, or for someone taking on a

00:12:38.691 --> 00:12:43.649
project that is completely novel for them. I have both a habit of finding and

00:12:43.649 --> 00:12:48.151
consuming new research materials, as well as existing data that can be

00:12:48.151 --> 00:12:52.913
cross-referenced with those inputs. My new widget will likely be iterated,

00:12:52.913 --> 00:12:57.545
designed, and rendered using the same pipeline, dozens of times, without

00:12:57.545 --> 00:13:02.633
approval. So my edits in the revision stage will also show up in the design and

00:13:02.633 --> 00:13:07.852
ideation stages. There would be a render2 stage that could be differentiated as a

00:13:07.852 --> 00:13:12.745
final version, that would likely go through additional finishing stages; but

00:13:12.745 --> 00:13:17.703
I could be rendering/making provisional versions of my widget within hours of

00:13:17.703 --> 00:13:22.400
brainstorming on my (continuing or purpose-accumulated) research. Most of

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:26.575
the time spent researching and brainstorming ideas is letting the

00:13:26.575 --> 00:13:31.533
information attach to other ideas I’ve stored in some ordered or casual spot;

00:13:31.533 --> 00:13:36.752
which is why I have to do it a little bit at a time, all the time. If I’ve worked

00:13:36.752 --> 00:13:41.776
on a similar widget before, my mind is already a magnet for information in the

00:13:41.776 --> 00:13:47.060
category—sucking things in that might be of use well before I commit to developing

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:51.757
a new one. But information is also ammunition, so pointing it at yourself

00:13:51.757 --> 00:13:53.453
can be a little dangerous.

00:13:53.453 --> 00:13:58.784
A lot of us are really impressed with complex, sophisticated work. On one edge

00:13:58.784 --> 00:14:03.907
is the incredibly time consuming; along another, is work that seems to flow

00:14:03.907 --> 00:14:08.684
effortlessly into complication. Especially in novels, good craft shows

00:14:08.684 --> 00:14:14.222
through illustration—we are shown what is important with skilled marks/phrases. I

00:14:14.222 --> 00:14:19.138
loved every single reference to pressures, and gauges, and gasses in the

00:14:19.138 --> 00:14:24.122
rockets of Thomas Pynchon’s novel Gravity’s Rainbow. They helped sell the

00:14:24.122 --> 00:14:29.661
revisionist pornanoia. The author came to the sources for that quite honestly; he

00:14:29.661 --> 00:14:35.130
studied physics at Cornell, he served in the Navy, and he was a technical writer

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:40.461
for Boeing. This fulfills the ‘write what you know’ maxim pretty well—but that

00:14:40.461 --> 00:14:45.445
doesn’t help so much with the narrative proper. Pynchon could know WW2 by

00:14:45.445 --> 00:14:50.638
reading, and from the generation just before him that fought through it, but

00:14:50.638 --> 00:14:55.830
that just provides the background. The foregrounds, the subjects drenched in

00:14:55.830 --> 00:15:01.161
thick, nerdy, erect detail, were lyric ooze. The specific and factual grounded

00:15:01.161 --> 00:15:06.422
the fantastic. Strip away the historical flashbacks, the science, and the war

00:15:06.422 --> 00:15:11.891
itself, and we are left with characters who have no motivation—breathing vacuum.

00:15:11.891 --> 00:15:17.291
So the sophistication works—it provides an integrated set of objects that can’t

00:15:17.291 --> 00:15:22.622
be removed without the entire structure crumbling. That can only be said about

00:15:22.622 --> 00:15:27.953
art of the very highest quality—but most of the work to the left of that curve

00:15:27.953 --> 00:15:33.353
could be disassociated and recombined ad nauseam. Most ornamentation is a moiré

00:15:33.353 --> 00:15:34.599
masking ugly form.

00:15:34.599 --> 00:15:39.309
My oscillating focus is on a fuzzy, idiosyncratic matrix of source

00:15:39.309 --> 00:15:44.743
documentation. What is not in the record is not in the world—but what is not

00:15:44.743 --> 00:15:50.394
efficiently tagged in the metadata may be found, in purgatory. My studio bursts

00:15:50.394 --> 00:15:55.611
with evidence of things that might one day make arguments or jewels. Each

00:15:55.611 --> 00:16:01.263
object, source, note, mutation, sketch, version, amputation, comment, abortion,

00:16:01.263 --> 00:16:06.335
junkheap, success is compulsively saved—though not necessarily with the

00:16:06.335 --> 00:16:12.203
flags that will allow me to catch back up to it later. The books in my library are

00:16:12.203 --> 00:16:16.768
among the most organized of these fonts; which is to say that my

00:16:16.768 --> 00:16:22.420
literature-galactica has expanded out into only about five places. They started

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:27.999
out on a single shelf, but cascaded into islands at each of the places where I

00:16:27.999 --> 00:16:33.433
might want to read or refer to things made of purple plastic, or examples of

00:16:33.433 --> 00:16:39.012
stainless stampings, or the supporting walls of crumbling art schools in Cuba.

00:16:39.012 --> 00:16:44.210
My collection of toys is an entropic corpus — what started as a small study

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:49.548
collection, related to toys about craft processes, grew manic as I started to

00:16:49.548 --> 00:16:54.535
consume the various versions that were produced throughout the twentieth

00:16:54.535 --> 00:16:59.732
century. And then I proceeded to adopt didactic toys in general, to acquire

00:16:59.732 --> 00:17:05.070
spare parts I might never need, and now they’re packed three layers deep — on

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:10.549
shelves that go from floor to ceiling in two rooms. The dark matter filling the

00:17:10.549 --> 00:17:15.957
gaps between the books and toys is made of old laptops, stethoscopes, boxes of

00:17:15.957 --> 00:17:21.084
test tubes, Trans Am parts, vintage software and industrial scraps (things

00:17:21.084 --> 00:17:26.493
that might be tools or found forms for new projects — most of which will never

00:17:26.493 --> 00:17:32.252
be used.) These storage habits bookend my widget making — a database queried during

00:17:32.252 --> 00:17:37.731
research phases. I build up my stores as I’m ideating and I pull or push at its

00:17:37.731 --> 00:17:43.420
margins every time I pass a thrift shop. Purges happen only when the shelves start

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:43.982
to bend.

00:17:43.982 --> 00:17:50.260
“Art is as notoriously resistant to both measurement and management as it is

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:52.106
powerful in its effect.”.

00:17:52.106 --> 00:17:59.815
Footnote 3. Stephen Bayley, Cars: Freedom, Style, Sex, Power, Motion,

00:17:59.815 --> 00:18:03.670
Colour, Everything, 2009, page 11.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:08.959
Alva Noë argued that art removes things from their expected settings and, “in

00:18:08.959 --> 00:18:14.387
making them strange,” we bring “out the ways and textures of the embedding that

00:18:14.387 --> 00:18:19.954
had been taken for granted. A work of art is a strange tool, an alien implement.”

00:18:19.954 --> 00:18:25.522
Design, on the other hand, generally aims to humanize the alien—to use process to

00:18:25.522 --> 00:18:30.532
investigate the person and deliver something surprising; pleasurable, but

00:18:30.532 --> 00:18:35.960
accessible. Design makes the machine seem to have been born. While it generally

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:41.319
hides the manipulation as well as fine art does, it is no less artificial. The

00:18:41.319 --> 00:18:46.469
products of our labor are all naturally synthetic; modern habits built on a

00:18:46.469 --> 00:18:50.018
million years of instinct pressed against invention.

00:18:50.018 --> 00:18:55.681
Our species creates order and stores it in grids, the most basic of which is the

00:18:55.681 --> 00:19:01.273
list. We inventory what we have to see what we are missing, to make a place for

00:19:01.273 --> 00:19:06.506
things that we might need later. “What does culture want? To make infinity

00:19:06.506 --> 00:19:11.954
comprehensible…we make lists because we don’t want to die.” So it probably is

00:19:11.954 --> 00:19:17.546
with creating anything new — we consume and make because we live. Our ideas are

00:19:17.546 --> 00:19:23.138
molded into things using a technology called organization. Entering data into a

00:19:23.138 --> 00:19:28.514
grid, reading the tables and pulling things out to place them next to things

00:19:28.514 --> 00:19:34.321
pulled from other sources is always done with low confidence but high consequence.

00:19:34.321 --> 00:19:39.885
Variations within an organized system can account for the compression of processes

00:19:39.885 --> 00:19:44.967
into kernels that encase, obscure, and support their code; allowing them to

00:19:44.967 --> 00:19:50.325
shrink until they seem automatic, or to inflate until each stage requires teams

00:19:50.325 --> 00:19:55.545
and meetings to handle the complexity. Look into the sky: our stars, planets,

00:19:55.545 --> 00:20:00.902
and galaxies appear to be moving—and in some ways, they ARE in motion. But what

00:20:00.902 --> 00:20:06.123
we see is largely the result of the motion of our planet; which is itself the

00:20:06.123 --> 00:20:11.480
result of the pull of our sun. The sun moves because of the pull of other stars

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:17.112
and galaxies, it pulls them—and all of it inflates under the influence of geometric

00:20:17.112 --> 00:20:18.486
and quantum gravity.

00:20:18.486 --> 00:20:23.521
Following my intuition, I might arrive at some particular mechanism for a new

00:20:23.521 --> 00:20:28.423
widget in a flash. Another way of saying that is: after years of looking at

00:20:28.423 --> 00:20:33.723
various mechanisms, and with the amount of experience I’ve accumulated working on

00:20:33.723 --> 00:20:39.023
similar projects, I had a new idea when I didn’t happen to be in my studio trying

00:20:39.023 --> 00:20:44.124
to solve that particular problem. Other mechanisms might be arrived at by more

00:20:44.124 --> 00:20:49.225
formally stepping through the design process. I might spend some time studying

00:20:49.225 --> 00:20:54.260
the requirements of the opening, even questioning whether a mechanism of that

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:58.964
type is even called for—or whether I can solve the problem in some other

00:20:58.964 --> 00:21:03.866
mechanical way? Then I can survey the products of other objects in the same

00:21:03.866 --> 00:21:08.702
class, the way I might mimic nature to solve such a problem, the available

00:21:08.702 --> 00:21:13.406
off-the-shelf solutions, custom materials, iterate through some ideas on

00:21:13.406 --> 00:21:18.640
paper, test the best of them, commit them to material, do some more testing, and

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:19.832
make changes, etc.

00:21:19.832 --> 00:21:24.546
Utilizing this design thinking says little about the quality of that

00:21:24.546 --> 00:21:29.681
thinking; I could have had a useful insight intuitively, or rigorously, or

00:21:29.681 --> 00:21:34.535
anywhere in between. The difference between intuition and a calculated

00:21:34.535 --> 00:21:40.023
solution can be as much about perspective and style as anything else; though we

00:21:40.023 --> 00:21:45.369
might have a semantic preference for procedural or metaphysical explanations.

00:21:45.369 --> 00:21:50.927
The primary advantage of the intuitive approach is that it sounds good, is fast,

00:21:50.927 --> 00:21:56.415
and might work well for some people who use it (probably individuals working on

00:21:56.415 --> 00:22:01.972
self-directed, speculative projects.) The primary advantage of a more formal and

00:22:01.972 --> 00:22:07.038
segmented approach is that it sounds good, is transparent, and might work

00:22:07.038 --> 00:22:11.681
better for some people and projects (probably teams.) And there are

00:22:11.681 --> 00:22:16.957
exceptions and variations and parallel systems of processes that may each be

00:22:16.957 --> 00:22:22.304
useful if they produce results that someone is willing to pay for or continue

00:22:22.304 --> 00:22:27.439
using. A way to assess this may be—was the intuition or process useful? In

00:22:27.439 --> 00:22:32.927
watching the stars move, did you identify the actual motion at work, or did you

00:22:32.927 --> 00:22:37.992
mistake the Earth for a stationary center—and what are the long and short

00:22:37.992 --> 00:22:40.173
term consequences of that view?

00:22:40.173 --> 00:22:45.911
If there is an opposite type to myself, it is whoever readily embraces the happy

00:22:45.911 --> 00:22:51.648
accident. I might make them, but I am not happy about them. I want to control my

00:22:51.648 --> 00:22:57.023
process, not have it control me. My system for accomplishing this, at least

00:22:57.023 --> 00:23:01.743
in my mind, is the illusion of independent variable control. It is

00:23:01.743 --> 00:23:06.319
limited control, sure—but with some interpolation, it is useful.

00:23:06.319 --> 00:23:11.802
“Not without deep sorrow do we admit to ourselves that artists of all times, at

00:23:11.802 --> 00:23:17.144
their most inspired, have transported to a heavenly transfiguration precisely

00:23:17.144 --> 00:23:22.486
those ideas that we now know to be false: artists glorify man’s religious and

00:23:22.486 --> 00:23:27.547
philosophical errors...” I work in the way that I do because I want to be

00:23:27.547 --> 00:23:32.960
involved with things that reflect my values, to make things that are examined,

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:38.724
innovative and beautiful. And I have come to think of the elimination of chance and

00:23:38.724 --> 00:23:44.347
mistakes as an ethic. But the more I look at the world, the more I read, the more

00:23:44.347 --> 00:23:49.830
people I talk to and the more I think about what I’m doing and ask myself why I

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:54.961
do it and what it means—the less convinced I am that what I see is what is

00:23:54.961 --> 00:23:59.952
there, the less convinced I am that anything I know is free of glorified

00:23:59.952 --> 00:24:05.083
error. Fine jewelers will often use reflective surfaces to distract people

00:24:05.083 --> 00:24:10.144
from the shortcomings in their work; art-jewelers try the same thing with

00:24:10.144 --> 00:24:15.276
sandblasters and dark patinas and generalized weirdness. Everyone has some

00:24:15.276 --> 00:24:20.407
way of mitigating weakness at some stage in their process. The crafting of

00:24:20.407 --> 00:24:26.030
concepts is perilous; the combinations of variables and operations, normal to one

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:31.373
another, outnumber animistic gods. The crafting of composition seems a little

00:24:31.373 --> 00:24:36.504
more stable. (Although calculating the bond dissociation strengths between

00:24:36.504 --> 00:24:41.565
certain formal and conceptual elements can be difficult.) The crafting of

00:24:41.565 --> 00:24:47.048
material seems relatively easy to control by comparison. Once silver is chosen,

00:24:47.048 --> 00:24:52.179
communicating anything that silver doesn’t say is improbable. Once casting

00:24:52.179 --> 00:24:57.802
is chosen, anything better said in sheet, tube, or forged rod will remain unsaid.

00:24:57.802 --> 00:25:03.426
No matter how many hammer marks we leave, no matter what we electroplate it with,

00:25:03.426 --> 00:25:07.573
no matter how well we polish it, it will always mean silver.

00:25:07.573 --> 00:25:11.753
There is no ne plus ultra, not even in the mind.

00:25:11.753 --> 00:25:17.187
Even surfaces are imperfect — they are all swirled and scratched, with seams and

00:25:17.187 --> 00:25:22.758
bubbles and pits — before you even get to the ugly choices their designers made in

00:25:22.758 --> 00:25:28.123
terms of color, pattern and intentionally imparted texture. Forms often perform

00:25:28.123 --> 00:25:33.488
poorly, or have reasonable utility while failing to signify their affordance to

00:25:33.488 --> 00:25:38.577
the user. Materials fail in sunlight, under stress, or to adequately engage

00:25:38.577 --> 00:25:43.667
people who might have fallen in love with them if the right matter had been

00:25:43.667 --> 00:25:48.619
located. But even when all these variables are exceptionally attended to,

00:25:48.619 --> 00:25:53.915
the premise that we build an object on can be flawed. That is, the content and

00:25:53.915 --> 00:25:59.005
context and purpose and history and influence and perspective of the things

00:25:59.005 --> 00:26:04.232
used as sources are as likely as the objects they inform to be ill-considered

00:26:04.232 --> 00:26:09.666
in subtle or serious ways. It may be that every source, when scrutinized closely

00:26:09.666 --> 00:26:14.480
enough or from far enough away, is questionable. And building on top of

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:19.226
these sources is an opportunity to explore, but also an opportunity to

00:26:19.226 --> 00:26:24.454
multiply the misunderstanding. Looking too deeply for meaning in our work and

00:26:24.454 --> 00:26:29.887
lives will always undermine it, bringing Sisyphus’ rock rolling back down on top

00:26:29.887 --> 00:26:33.395
of us. Meaning in our work is relative, provisional.

00:26:33.395 --> 00:26:39.111
“Error does not carry any recognizable badge, for when we change our point of

00:26:39.111 --> 00:26:43.562
view, to focus on a different problem, what had been error may become

00:26:43.562 --> 00:26:47.556
information, and what had been information may become error.”.

00:26:47.556 --> 00:26:57.541
Footnote 4. Solomon Diamond, Information and Error, Basic Books, 1959, page 7.

00:26:57.541 --> 00:27:03.246
Consider a cruciform pendant: no form or story, material or finish can rescue such

00:27:03.246 --> 00:27:08.670
a thing from the lack of any empirical basis for belief in the divinity of the

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:13.670
Christ concept. So the mental and verbal symbol, as well as its material

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:19.446
fabrication in jewelry or other forms, is laminated in this basic flaw. We can talk

00:27:19.446 --> 00:27:24.729
about how well it communicates this common myth, how innovative it is within

00:27:24.729 --> 00:27:29.307
the niche and beyond it. But we are building on apocrypha; we will

00:27:29.307 --> 00:27:35.012
misunderstand one or more interpretations of a gospel and we will apply our flawed

00:27:35.012 --> 00:27:40.435
retelling in a way that doesn’t serve the symbolic ends that either the dogma,

00:27:40.435 --> 00:27:46.000
designer, or potential wearer requires if they look closely enough. All of these

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:50.507
can be evaluated; factually, aesthetically, and in terms of their

00:27:50.507 --> 00:27:56.213
utility (to the individual, to the small and large group, to their god idea and to

00:27:56.213 --> 00:28:01.777
the planet,) but they won’t be able to iron out the basic problems in either the

00:28:01.777 --> 00:28:07.552
referent or references. It will always be difficult to apply two-state truth-values

00:28:07.552 --> 00:28:12.342
to our evaluations; they will be in between wrong and right—sometimes

00:28:12.342 --> 00:28:18.118
simultaneously occupying multiple states, depending on our perspectives on a single

00:28:18.118 --> 00:28:23.682
facet. But a cross won’t even come close to satisfying our interest in truth. It

00:28:23.682 --> 00:28:28.753
is an overtly mythological totem, but maybe its premise being in error is

00:28:28.753 --> 00:28:30.514
specific to its category.

00:28:30.514 --> 00:28:34.866
A decade ago, I stood in the studio of one of the most creative and

00:28:34.866 --> 00:28:40.142
well-respected artists in my field, and they graciously brought out and showed us

00:28:40.142 --> 00:28:45.153
decades worth of their work. It was beautiful, well-made, surprising in scale

00:28:45.153 --> 00:28:50.033
and material. They told us about the emotional and narrative content of the

00:28:50.033 --> 00:28:54.386
work; and my appreciation waned. It wasn’t that they weren’t a good

00:28:54.386 --> 00:28:59.331
storyteller, it’s that what they were showing and what they were saying were

00:28:59.331 --> 00:29:04.277
hopelessly incongruous. They thought the work was funny, but it was patently

00:29:04.277 --> 00:29:08.893
depressing. It was absurd, but not humorously. Because it was art, it’s

00:29:08.893 --> 00:29:14.235
probably easy to dismiss this as being a natural part of subjectivity. But, to the

00:29:14.235 --> 00:29:19.444
extent that art is about communication, it seemed fairly obvious to me that even

00:29:19.444 --> 00:29:24.654
the best among us occasionally don’t know what our images mean to the people who

00:29:24.654 --> 00:29:25.313
read them.

00:29:25.313 --> 00:29:30.562
Roads are paved error. They freeze and thaw, crack and break open to swallow us.

00:29:30.562 --> 00:29:35.413
Even when they are in good condition they are often crowded, or undergoing

00:29:35.413 --> 00:29:40.862
improvements that will soon disintegrate. They are amazing feats of engineering and

00:29:40.862 --> 00:29:46.178
labor, but they are also mechanisms that enable suburban flight. They can destroy

00:29:46.178 --> 00:29:51.162
habitats and they tend to be designed only for motor vehicles. To the extent

00:29:51.162 --> 00:29:56.345
that they are good for the latter they are not equally appropriate for vehicles

00:29:56.345 --> 00:30:01.395
of various sizes, and little systemic work is done to keep animals of various

00:30:01.395 --> 00:30:06.777
sizes from venturing into them. They are useful and destructive, a technology that

00:30:06.777 --> 00:30:11.694
enables a civilization that is itself compelling and violent, beautiful and

00:30:11.694 --> 00:30:16.811
unfair. Roads allow a type of freedom and adventure, and they underwrite whole

00:30:16.811 --> 00:30:21.927
segments of our economy. One might be better than another by various measures,

00:30:21.927 --> 00:30:26.844
but they are all expensive, regressive things that allow people to drive in

00:30:26.844 --> 00:30:31.695
cars, enabling a significant amount of waste, property loss, suffering and

00:30:31.695 --> 00:30:36.812
death, as well as safety, speed, economy and possibility. But they are easy to

00:30:36.812 --> 00:30:41.995
excuse; maybe because we’ve been building on top of simple trails for hundreds,

00:30:41.995 --> 00:30:43.656
even thousands, of years.

00:30:43.656 --> 00:30:48.604
Our food is a similar mix of modernity and tradition, though far simpler (at

00:30:48.604 --> 00:30:53.551
least physically) to rehabilitate or rebuild from scratch than roads. Eating

00:30:53.551 --> 00:30:58.499
is a more fundamental human need than driving, so criticism of a staple will

00:30:58.499 --> 00:31:03.182
quickly run into arguments about instinct. Ice cream, on the other hand,

00:31:03.182 --> 00:31:08.328
is obviously fairly modern — and eaten purely for pleasure. It is made from the

00:31:08.328 --> 00:31:13.077
milk of cows who are kept forcibly impregnated most of their lives before

00:31:13.077 --> 00:31:17.827
going to slaughter. Their daughters suffer the same fate. Their sons (not

00:31:17.827 --> 00:31:22.840
having been bred to become enough ‘beef’) go into sheds for a few months, are

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:27.986
deprived exercise and are slaughtered to become veal. This milk is trucked to a

00:31:27.986 --> 00:31:32.406
plant where it is mixed with sugar; frozen; mixed with salt, ice and

00:31:32.406 --> 00:31:37.287
emulsifiers; packaged; trucked to a store; purchased; rushed home in a car;

00:31:37.287 --> 00:31:42.301
kept frozen in an electric freezer…until people eat it as a luxury that is no

00:31:42.301 --> 00:31:47.710
longer recognizable as milk, sugar, salt, and refrigeration. Because it is entirely

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:52.591
wasteful, save the possible beneficial effect of increasing the movement of

00:31:52.591 --> 00:31:57.869
trade in the economy (so that more people can consume ice cream) it’s hard to see

00:31:57.869 --> 00:32:03.212
something of this type as being composed of anything but a bright flaw. Unless you

00:32:03.212 --> 00:32:07.434
look at it only from the point of view of the ice cream partisan.

00:32:07.434 --> 00:32:12.441
Take a minute, open a few tabs in your web browser, and perform some Google

00:32:12.441 --> 00:32:17.515
Image searches for a 1974 Volkswagen Golf, DeVos Place, and Rodin’s Gates of

00:32:17.515 --> 00:32:22.861
Hell. They are in marginally different domains, each is loved by many people who

00:32:22.861 --> 00:32:28.003
are familiar with them, and they can each be understood without difficulty on

00:32:28.003 --> 00:32:33.348
multiple levels. They are each resonant, original forms; each perform particular

00:32:33.348 --> 00:32:38.828
utilitarian and symbolic tasks, and each is as flawed as it is triumphant. I’m not

00:32:38.828 --> 00:32:43.497
going to articulate all of the questionable features and histories and

00:32:43.497 --> 00:32:49.045
shapes of each, just to call out a few of the issues that come immediately to mind.

00:32:49.045 --> 00:32:54.395
The 1974 Golf is the GenX hipster dream — it was a fun, practical, affordable,

00:32:54.395 --> 00:32:59.815
sporty, beachy, car that played minor roles in the mid 80’s films Thrashin’ and

00:32:59.815 --> 00:33:05.235
Can’t Buy Me Love. Its wedgy shape was designed by the same guy that eventually

00:33:05.235 --> 00:33:10.377
penned the DeLorean, Giorgetto Giugiaro. One of the many terms I have heard

00:33:10.377 --> 00:33:15.797
friends use about this car is that it’s minimal — it’s simple, boxy and doesn’t

00:33:15.797 --> 00:33:21.287
have a lot of the distractions of modern cars. And the boxiness was a trait that

00:33:21.287 --> 00:33:26.706
had some legs. It’s still seen, from the Scion XB to the Hummer to a Volvo. But

00:33:26.706 --> 00:33:31.709
another way of looking at its simplicity is that it was cheap, feeble and

00:33:31.709 --> 00:33:36.712
disposable. Some of these flaws are actually the charm that people see in

00:33:36.712 --> 00:33:41.576
them; they have the quirky, retro featureless features that I like in a

00:33:41.576 --> 00:33:47.066
manual typewriter. Some of my VW loving friends hate my favorite domestic brand,

00:33:47.066 --> 00:33:52.138
General Motors, largely because of the poor performing cars GM built while

00:33:52.138 --> 00:33:57.489
companies like VW were blowing up in our market. History matters. GM made some

00:33:57.489 --> 00:34:02.770
crap cars, VW made some good cars — and brand loyalty was born. In argument I

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:07.981
can’t really use the fact that the many Pontiac Fieros I’ve owned have never

00:34:07.981 --> 00:34:13.471
caught fire to convince people that they should buy a used or new GM car. I have

00:34:13.471 --> 00:34:18.891
multiple friends who have early and late model VWs that they own because of the

00:34:18.891 --> 00:34:24.171
historical cuteness of cars like the Golf, and their perceptions of companies

00:34:24.171 --> 00:34:29.313
like GM as big, lazy, low quality polluters. They see VW (and BMW, Mercedes

00:34:29.313 --> 00:34:34.594
and Volvo) as an innovative, modern, fast, high-tech and high-value company —

00:34:34.594 --> 00:34:40.292
characteristics that we would all like to relate to. But that’s not what I see when

00:34:40.292 --> 00:34:45.712
I see a VW. Volkswagen’s and Porsche’s founder was a fervent Nazi. The Beetle’s

00:34:45.712 --> 00:34:51.202
design brief was written by Adolf Hitler and the first versions were built using

00:34:51.202 --> 00:34:56.413
Jewish and Gypsy slaves. While I am writing this, Volkswagen is embroiled in

00:34:56.413 --> 00:35:01.624
a scandal wherein they intentionally cheated on emissions tests in order not

00:35:01.624 --> 00:35:06.627
just to pass them, but to trick their customers into believing that their

00:35:06.627 --> 00:35:12.047
vehicles were exceptionally clean. We don’t just act in accordance with our gut

00:35:12.047 --> 00:35:17.259
on these things, we choose what it important to us and make decisions — each

00:35:17.259 --> 00:35:22.679
of which involve compromises of various kinds and which could be looked at from

00:35:22.679 --> 00:35:26.292
different angles, with each showing different truths.

00:35:26.292 --> 00:35:31.562
I was in Grand Rapids in 2002, as the DeVos Place convention center was being

00:35:31.562 --> 00:35:36.831
constructed along the Grand River, a few blocks from where I teach. Its grand

00:35:36.831 --> 00:35:42.032
opening was on Dec 20, 2003, but wasn’t complete until early in 2005. It was

00:35:42.032 --> 00:35:46.816
exciting to have a large exhibit hall, capable of hosting a small auto

00:35:46.816 --> 00:35:52.294
exhibition handed down from the monster auto show in Detroit, held weeks before.

00:35:52.294 --> 00:35:57.286
A little less exciting is the name; a branding opportunity for one of the

00:35:57.286 --> 00:36:02.902
right-wing billionaires in the region who founded Amway. DeVos Place has an atrium

00:36:02.902 --> 00:36:08.103
that is almost beautiful—with a winding series of planar chandeliers cutting

00:36:08.103 --> 00:36:13.095
through a multi-story opening with a large walkway that looks down on the

00:36:13.095 --> 00:36:18.157
convention floor. But there is something awkward about it. Grand Rapids is

00:36:18.157 --> 00:36:23.080
waveform-crazy—much of what’s been built in the past 20 years has had an

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:28.488
undulating water motif; from the city’s graphic design to its suburban airport,

00:36:28.488 --> 00:36:33.550
from the research hospital on top of Michigan Avenue to the DeVos Place on

00:36:33.550 --> 00:36:39.097
Monroe Street. If you look at the images of the DVP in your browser tab, the main

00:36:39.097 --> 00:36:44.020
form is a tense wave with a horrid kink at the top southwest edge as the

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:49.359
convention space transitions into the atrium. It looks like half a fish with a

00:36:49.359 --> 00:36:54.628
broken neck. The DVP and its related venues serve the needs of the community,

00:36:54.628 --> 00:36:59.759
so far as I can tell. And it isn’t horribly ugly by local standards. But if

00:36:59.759 --> 00:37:04.682
you look a little closer, it’s a cable-tied, typographically challenged,

00:37:04.682 --> 00:37:09.883
visual mess of a complex. This is, seemingly, surface error: just the shapes

00:37:09.883 --> 00:37:15.083
and skins are ill-considered. No big deal. But given that a building of this

00:37:15.083 --> 00:37:20.561
kind has an outsized effect on the way this community is seen by those traveling

00:37:20.561 --> 00:37:25.622
here for conferences—the amateur styling reinforces perceptions of Midwest

00:37:25.622 --> 00:37:26.385
mediocrity.

00:37:26.385 --> 00:37:31.962
Auguste Rodin worked on his commission for the Gates of Hell for almost forty

00:37:31.962 --> 00:37:37.685
years, dying before he finished them. When the first casting from the model was

00:37:37.685 --> 00:37:43.555
displayed in the Musee Rodin, they were thirty-four years late for the opening of

00:37:43.555 --> 00:37:49.205
a decorative arts museum that was never built. Its central figure, the Poet or

00:37:49.205 --> 00:37:55.001
Thinker, has become far more familiar to us than the sculptural menagerie it was

00:37:55.001 --> 00:38:00.651
crafted for. The gates are, at least in part, a meditation on, or illustration

00:38:00.651 --> 00:38:06.668
of, a set of gates in the Inferno—part of Dante Alighieri’s medieval epic poem, the

00:38:06.668 --> 00:38:11.951
Divine Comedy. On Dante’s gates are inscribed the words, as translated by

00:38:11.951 --> 00:38:14.079
Longfellow in the late 1800s:

00:38:14.079 --> 00:38:17.330
“Justice incited my sublime Creator;

00:38:17.330 --> 00:38:19.698
Created me divine Omnipotence,

00:38:19.698 --> 00:38:22.577
The highest Wisdom and the primal Love.

00:38:22.577 --> 00:38:25.317
Before me there were no created things,

00:38:25.317 --> 00:38:28.614
Only eterne, and I eternal last.

00:38:28.614 --> 00:38:32.329
All hope abandon, ye who enter in!”.

00:38:32.329 --> 00:38:37.902
And yet, H.F. Cary translated the same low Italian in the early 1800s as:

00:38:37.902 --> 00:38:40.921
“Justice, the founder of my fabric, mov’d:

00:38:40.921 --> 00:38:43.522
To rear me was the task of power divine,

00:38:43.522 --> 00:38:47.841
Supremest wisdom, and primeval love.

00:38:47.841 --> 00:38:50.163
Before me things create were none,

00:38:50.163 --> 00:38:53.739
…save things eternal, and eternal I endure.

00:38:53.739 --> 00:38:57.640
All hope abandon, ye who enter here!”.

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:03.309
There are scholars of Italian, Medieval literature and Dante who argue there are

00:39:03.309 --> 00:39:08.333
subtly different ways to take in the relative meanings of Dante’s short

00:39:08.333 --> 00:39:13.357
summary, “Lasciate ogne speranza, voi ch’intrate.” By the middle of the

00:39:13.357 --> 00:39:19.098
twentieth century Dorothy Sayers said it was “Lay down all hope, all you who that

00:39:19.098 --> 00:39:24.265
go in by me.” Are these differences stylistic or substantive? What is the

00:39:24.265 --> 00:39:29.719
difference when it comes to poetry? We can imagine that this kind of thing is

00:39:29.719 --> 00:39:35.317
common to translation work in general; languages didn’t evolve in a manner that

00:39:35.317 --> 00:39:40.125
permits direct substitution of even words, let alone the meanings of

00:39:40.125 --> 00:39:45.722
sentences and paragraphs—and certainly not when those meanings are infused with

00:39:45.722 --> 00:39:48.306
the rhythm of verse, rhyming or not.

00:39:48.306 --> 00:39:54.515
Rodin also worked from the relatively modern Baudelaire. In "Hymn to Beauty,"

00:39:54.515 --> 00:39:57.130
from "Flowers of Evil," B wrote:

00:39:57.130 --> 00:40:00.799
“From God or Satan? Angel, Mermaid, Proserpine?

00:40:00.799 --> 00:40:04.421
What matter if thou makest — blithe, voluptuous sprite —,

00:40:04.421 --> 00:40:08.229
With rhythms, perfumes, visions—O, mine ONLY queen!

00:40:08.229 --> 00:40:12.223
The universe less hideous and the hours less trite”.

00:40:12.223 --> 00:40:17.266
What did Rodin make of the original French version of this collection of

00:40:17.266 --> 00:40:22.380
poems? How did his attraction to Baudelaire’s work alloy with his reading

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:27.920
of Dante? And besides these references, what else was he looking at, who was he

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:33.247
speaking with, about what? How many of these things found their way into the

00:40:33.247 --> 00:40:38.431
density of his gates? And how many of them, from our point of view or from

00:40:38.431 --> 00:40:44.255
imagined perspectives in Rodin’s day, are logically bulletproof? Assuming they were

00:40:44.255 --> 00:40:49.298
tight, within their metaphysical domains anyway, how many of them became

00:40:49.298 --> 00:40:54.838
faithfully appropriated source materials in that they were represented in a way

00:40:54.838 --> 00:41:00.378
that corresponds to their original or translated meanings? Or were they used as

00:41:00.378 --> 00:41:05.563
antitheses for some portions, tensions off of which figures appeared to do

00:41:05.563 --> 00:41:11.103
dialectic battle? And then there is the quality of the castings, the patina, or

00:41:11.103 --> 00:41:16.430
the placement—most of which was done after the artist died (though using his

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:22.112
wishes and his previous work as guides.) How do the choices made in each of those

00:41:22.112 --> 00:41:27.368
areas, along with the themes and intents, contribute to the accuracy of the

00:41:27.368 --> 00:41:32.766
gates—is it the translation of poetic ideas of hell to people walking through

00:41:32.766 --> 00:41:33.121
them?

00:41:33.121 --> 00:41:38.274
The windows in the house where I live now are significantly better than the windows

00:41:38.274 --> 00:41:43.176
we had in the house I grew up in. More and bigger windows are better, right? If

00:41:43.176 --> 00:41:48.078
you live within sight of trees you want to see them, and windows seem like they

00:41:48.078 --> 00:41:52.917
are more efficient than light bulbs—the more windows, the more light. They are

00:41:52.917 --> 00:41:57.253
metaphors of transparency and modernity—the very building block of the

00:41:57.253 --> 00:42:02.029
marquee Apple Stores. The ones I am looking at are all double pane, have very

00:42:02.029 --> 00:42:06.805
sturdy trim, let in light in just the right places, and are in sizes that are

00:42:06.805 --> 00:42:11.895
easy to find reasonably priced shades for when we don’t want light or eyes looking

00:42:11.895 --> 00:42:16.985
in. But they will lose their argon gas as the seals slowly crack. We often fail to

00:42:16.985 --> 00:42:22.013
open the right ones at the right times to balance the entering sunlight in winter

00:42:22.013 --> 00:42:27.040
versus the cold escaping in summer due to the relatively low insulation. And they

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:31.816
are significantly more expensive and wasteful to manufacture and replace than

00:42:31.816 --> 00:42:36.215
their timber and drywall based alternatives. Different glazing could be

00:42:36.215 --> 00:42:40.803
chosen, but there will always be a tradeoff for cost, efficiency, defense,

00:42:40.803 --> 00:42:41.620
or longevity.

00:42:41.620 --> 00:42:46.436
Do any of these examples really prove that everything is in a state of error

00:42:46.436 --> 00:42:51.574
and imperfection, or just that I am being picky and judgmental? How about looking

00:42:51.574 --> 00:42:52.858
at something I love?

00:42:52.858 --> 00:42:57.783
I own a Michael Graves can opener that I bought at Target in 2000. It is the

00:42:57.783 --> 00:43:02.708
perfect weight, easy to use, it has never broken or failed to open a can, is

00:43:02.708 --> 00:43:07.961
attractive, in a color that I love, feels great in the hand, is reasonably priced

00:43:07.961 --> 00:43:12.689
and is in my kitchen until I find something better—at which point it will

00:43:12.689 --> 00:43:17.943
go on the shelf in my studio. But a can opener assumes a can; a can assumes mines

00:43:17.943 --> 00:43:23.130
pulling iron, nickel and chromium out of the earth; a smelting plant pouring out

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:28.252
ingots and rolling them into sheets of steel; each making mountains of waste in

00:43:28.252 --> 00:43:33.440
runoff and depleted coal. Those sheets will be rolled into tubes, factories will

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:38.299
pack foodstuffs into them (some of which is likely the product of human and

00:43:38.299 --> 00:43:42.896
non-human suffering.) Trucks will carry the cans to a marketplace, that

00:43:42.896 --> 00:43:48.281
marketplace will heat and cool them until we come to collect them by spending money

00:43:48.281 --> 00:43:53.140
we earned doing tasks that do not challenge or nurture us to buy them. This

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:58.196
is before we even get to how the can opener itself is made, how we got the can

00:43:58.196 --> 00:44:03.187
to our kitchen, how the food will be prepared and what will happen to it when

00:44:03.187 --> 00:44:07.718
our bodies have stripped the nutrients we can use from their contents.

00:44:07.718 --> 00:44:13.348
Imagine a flowchart that diagrams each of these variables and the various decisions

00:44:13.348 --> 00:44:18.154
that need to be made in order to determine what, as well as whether and

00:44:18.154 --> 00:44:23.373
how, to do once we arrive at answers for can-based questions. For each of the

00:44:23.373 --> 00:44:28.797
variables I’ve listed, there are likely many dependent questions and conditions.

00:44:28.797 --> 00:44:34.015
Let’s assume an input statement of “I’m hungry,” or “I’m going to be hungry;”

00:44:34.015 --> 00:44:39.233
even those can be critically examined. “Are you sure?” “What’s your BMI?” “Do

00:44:39.233 --> 00:44:44.452
you deserve food?” “Will feeding yourself contribute to personal goals or the

00:44:44.452 --> 00:44:49.327
greater good?” And while some suggest binary answers, all of them can be

00:44:49.327 --> 00:44:54.064
answered in ways that have either low confidence or otherwise send the

00:44:54.064 --> 00:44:59.351
deliberation mechanism into an infinite loop. There are principles of excluded

00:44:59.351 --> 00:45:04.089
thirds to consider, logically; but these are measures of propositional

00:45:04.089 --> 00:45:09.170
imperfection—not of the number of possible ways to evaluate the world. Most

00:45:09.170 --> 00:45:14.731
of us will avoid this perilous complexity by answering with our gut. But every one

00:45:14.731 --> 00:45:19.743
of the assumptional errors in the decision we are crafting around is still

00:45:19.743 --> 00:45:24.893
there; threatening to make the whole thing crumple. It’s a set of relatively

00:45:24.893 --> 00:45:29.768
fine distinctions that could go still finer, but each set is evidence of

00:45:29.768 --> 00:45:34.986
imperfection—of unwhole understandability on a continuum between marginal and

00:45:34.986 --> 00:45:35.398
total.

00:45:35.398 --> 00:45:40.611
There are comedies, tragedies and dramas of error on everything worth recognizing.

00:45:40.611 --> 00:45:45.566
If not in the conception, then in the application. If not in the construction,

00:45:45.566 --> 00:45:50.457
then in the use. If not in the acting, then in the script. If not the script,

00:45:50.457 --> 00:45:54.962
then the playwright. If not the playwright, the stage, the theater, the

00:45:54.962 --> 00:45:59.788
entrance, the carpet… And these things are the play too—except to the extent

00:45:59.788 --> 00:46:02.427
that we can suspend our disbelief in them.

00:46:02.427 --> 00:46:07.947
What is the veracity of a wild tree or a flower or a beaver? Probably, so long as

00:46:07.947 --> 00:46:12.709
they were not cultivated or manipulated by man, they are impervious to

00:46:12.709 --> 00:46:17.884
examinations of beauty, utility, or meaning—except as would fit a particular

00:46:17.884 --> 00:46:23.128
human purpose. We build machines, and they sometimes fail to live up to their

00:46:23.128 --> 00:46:28.511
specifications—nature, not as much. We could weigh it or put a ruler up next to

00:46:28.511 --> 00:46:33.755
something in nature, but what would we be comparing it to? One thing could be

00:46:33.755 --> 00:46:38.862
taller, heavier than the next—but what would that mean? While there will be

00:46:38.862 --> 00:46:43.968
various ways that their genes may be expressed, with both idiosyncratic and

00:46:43.968 --> 00:46:49.350
disfiguring mutations, I am not able to come up with a theory by which cellular

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:54.802
things could be given truth-values that wouldn’t be humancentric; specifications

00:46:54.802 --> 00:47:00.115
set by the universe that have failed to be lived up to. The same would be true

00:47:00.115 --> 00:47:05.221
about wind, lakes, mountains, and planets—their fitness for a task can only

00:47:05.221 --> 00:47:10.811
be judged in relation to something in our minds. These kinds of sources are likely

00:47:10.811 --> 00:47:16.124
only capable of misleading us the moment we start integrating them into logos,

00:47:16.124 --> 00:47:20.955
metaphors, or aluminum alloy wheel designs—when we try to make sense of

00:47:20.955 --> 00:47:26.130
them. Hobbes said that “Nature itself cannot err,” which sounds about right.

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:31.581
What is susceptible to error, in these cases, isn’t so much the referent but the

00:47:31.581 --> 00:47:36.895
reference—the mental and physical links and morphings that happen as something

00:47:36.895 --> 00:47:40.138
crosses the transom from its province into ours.

00:47:40.138 --> 00:47:45.169
Love is another special case. My daughter, Memo, was ten weeks old when I

00:47:45.169 --> 00:47:50.410
brought her home from the Ann Arbor Humane Society, nearly twelve years ago.

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:55.581
She grew to sixty-five pounds and has been very healthy and gracious to the

00:47:55.581 --> 00:48:00.961
other animals that my wife and I have brought into our house over the years. I

00:48:00.961 --> 00:48:06.481
would stab someone in the face if they tried to hurt her—which is another way to

00:48:06.481 --> 00:48:11.652
say that I love her as much as I am capable of loving anyone. Can that love

00:48:11.652 --> 00:48:17.102
be queried in a way that could show its deficiency? If I were to use her as the

00:48:17.102 --> 00:48:22.203
source of a poem, could that source be shown to have a flawed premise? She

00:48:22.203 --> 00:48:27.654
exists, I say I love her, I could provide evidence of each thing, and love is a

00:48:27.654 --> 00:48:32.615
common input for many creative activities. Love could be flawed—that is,

00:48:32.615 --> 00:48:37.856
it might be comparably small, large, useless, or destructive. It has greater

00:48:37.856 --> 00:48:43.166
immunity to argument than the something we made that could be measured with a

00:48:43.166 --> 00:48:47.918
reliable gauge, but because the love exists in our minds, it could be

00:48:47.918 --> 00:48:53.088
measured—and could be usefully wrong to the level of confidence that we can

00:48:53.088 --> 00:48:55.045
measure any idea or emotion.

00:48:55.045 --> 00:49:00.637
From some vantage, nearly everything that humans make or participate in has a

00:49:00.637 --> 00:49:06.375
footprint that is larger, or deeper, or differently shaped than we might prefer

00:49:06.375 --> 00:49:11.820
if we were designing them in a clean room as deus ex machinas. This kind of

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:17.559
truth—that our source materials are as imperfect as we are—is sometimes used as

00:49:17.559 --> 00:49:23.445
an excuse not to exercise control—to bend to nihilism. Carbon emissions from cars

00:49:23.445 --> 00:49:28.815
come to mind—a simplistic argument deflects all attempts at reform because

00:49:28.815 --> 00:49:34.481
there are no perfect systems (no sources of energy that do no damage.) None of

00:49:34.481 --> 00:49:39.778
this is to say that we need look for the perfect. While verifiability and

00:49:39.778 --> 00:49:45.149
repeatability are important ideas in engineering and science—even they can

00:49:45.149 --> 00:49:50.446
acknowledge and systematize variation of certain kinds. But they start by

00:49:50.446 --> 00:49:56.185
recognizing it, and then decide where their tolerances can and ought to be set.

00:49:56.185 --> 00:50:01.777
The things we haven’t or can’t control for, in creative activities, are often

00:50:01.777 --> 00:50:06.191
embraced—from useful noise to the subject of the work itself.

00:50:06.191 --> 00:50:08.467
We are enterprises of error.

00:50:08.467 --> 00:50:13.800
Reducing error in perception and action to its minimum possible level is the

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:18.991
subject of many books on ethics, efficiency, and education. The economy of

00:50:18.991 --> 00:50:24.111
errors that I’m talking about isn’t about industrial quality or safety or

00:50:24.111 --> 00:50:29.658
yield—it’s about fog. Every thing is made from other things, and we utilize our

00:50:29.658 --> 00:50:35.133
technologies to make the most of these compounds and fabrications. But we tend

00:50:35.133 --> 00:50:39.897
to see these procecsses only in the logical, functional, mechanistic

00:50:39.897 --> 00:50:45.017
relationships that entangle them with our needs. We can occasionally work

00:50:45.017 --> 00:50:50.848
production backward and embrace a ‘cradle to grave’ ecology. But we can’t as easily

00:50:50.848 --> 00:50:55.186
see how the things we can’t control contribute to its meaning.

00:50:55.186 --> 00:51:00.528
The vision we lack isn’t optical, it’s cerebral. We tend to see in things what

00:51:00.528 --> 00:51:05.593
good writers and designers want us to—form, function, beauty. But we can’t

00:51:05.593 --> 00:51:10.797
see the misty contributions of chaos, the numbing outcomes of the vapid, the

00:51:10.797 --> 00:51:16.278
infantilizing influence of the bold, the nostalgic basis of waste, the men doing

00:51:16.278 --> 00:51:21.065
horrible things to one another, the demoralizing theft of novelty, the

00:51:21.065 --> 00:51:26.616
frustrations of choice and scarcity, the relationships between our noble work and

00:51:26.616 --> 00:51:31.958
the crimes of those we support with it, or how the ugly affects our individual

00:51:31.958 --> 00:51:36.954
and social psyches. Our work has causes and effects, as well as miasmatic

00:51:36.954 --> 00:51:41.394
interdependencies among what we do, why we love, and when we are.

00:51:41.394 --> 00:51:48.402
“First, what is the real nature of synthesis? And then, what is the real

00:51:48.402 --> 00:51:50.168
nature of control?”.

00:51:50.168 --> 00:51:56.268
Footnote 5. Thomas Pynchon, Gravity’s Rainbow, Viking Press, 1973, page 167.

00:51:56.268 --> 00:52:02.449
Pynchon’s context in GR is significantly different than I am hoping to elicit

00:52:02.449 --> 00:52:08.875
here—but these two questions seem apt in determinations of how we understand and

00:52:08.875 --> 00:52:15.382
mix sources into the work we do—and the extent to which we have dominion over the

00:52:15.382 --> 00:52:19.611
work product, and then responsibility for the source.

00:52:19.611 --> 00:52:24.961
Our objects are carriers of our Virtue and our Failure, and the analog states

00:52:24.961 --> 00:52:30.733
between V and F. But they carry each only so long as we continue to recognize them.

00:52:30.733 --> 00:52:36.154
When the light is on them, objects are base materials of sources, which become

00:52:36.154 --> 00:52:41.644
bodies, which influence us, that we use as tools to build yet more objects… But

00:52:41.644 --> 00:52:46.642
what if the interconnected ideas we build things on top of are mythical,

00:52:46.642 --> 00:52:52.063
incomplete, or false? What if the truths that we put at the center of our work

00:52:52.063 --> 00:52:56.075
turn out to be untruths, shallow impressions, or even deep

00:52:56.075 --> 00:53:01.566
un-understandings? What would that mean for the work? If we accept imperfection

00:53:01.566 --> 00:53:07.057
at the surface of an object, like cracks and color and texture variegation on a

00:53:07.057 --> 00:53:12.759
raku pot, would we dismiss whole vessels if a premise we based it on turned out to

00:53:12.759 --> 00:53:15.293
be confused, misleading, or corrupt?

00:53:15.293 --> 00:53:20.677
This might not be the kind of error we regret, the kind of thing that blows

00:53:20.677 --> 00:53:26.280
bubbles into our concrete (eventually allowing the building to fail.) It might

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:31.373
be a kind of reinforcement (or necessary) sweeming-inert aggregate. Our

00:53:31.373 --> 00:53:36.540
imperfection-infused products are evidence that our confidence intervals

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:37.631
are never 100%.

00:53:37.631 --> 00:53:43.028
Everything we make is flawed, imperfect, incomplete; physically and semantically.

00:53:43.028 --> 00:53:48.020
Not because we have a perfect matrix to compare a thing to, nor necessarily

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:52.743
because we have faith in inerrant c/Creators who have convinced us that

00:53:52.743 --> 00:53:57.667
everything is sin; but because our makings commit ideas to material. These

00:53:57.667 --> 00:54:03.132
ideas, these more perfectable cousins of our hypererrant crafting, are spelled out

00:54:03.132 --> 00:54:08.057
in words, phrases, and narratives. An errant verbal idea can be changed in

00:54:08.057 --> 00:54:12.644
scale or applicability, is provisional, can be updated to reflect new

00:54:12.644 --> 00:54:17.771
information, or can even eradicated should we find it opposes truth (although

00:54:17.771 --> 00:54:22.628
it can be extremely difficult.) Things too can be updated, but with great

00:54:22.628 --> 00:54:27.621
difficulty when compared to changing something that isn’t made of atoms put

00:54:27.621 --> 00:54:32.950
together with our hands and tools. To the extent that my can opener changes from

00:54:32.950 --> 00:54:38.212
being designed to open cans of dead fish into a sculpture (when it is no longer

00:54:38.212 --> 00:54:43.474
useful) it does so first in my mind. We pass over or include the errors we find

00:54:43.474 --> 00:54:48.399
into our thing-concepts. We pack new knowledge into old ideas and fix them

00:54:48.399 --> 00:54:53.324
into things that are as valid as we are capable of at the instant of their

00:54:53.324 --> 00:54:53.931
creation.

00:54:53.931 --> 00:54:57.934
The things we build are worth considering—occasionally

00:54:57.934 --> 00:55:03.750
celebrating—despite being the product of cruelty, ill performance, or profound

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:07.677
misunderstanding. Or perhaps because of these things.

00:55:07.677 --> 00:55:12.901
When I fail to choose all the right variables, and the piece fails to be the

00:55:12.901 --> 00:55:18.405
shiny, perfect thing that I imagined it to be, whom do I blame? I might blame my

00:55:18.405 --> 00:55:23.838
parents, my children, my car, my job, or even my tools or materials. But we can

00:55:23.838 --> 00:55:28.993
all recognize that no one else should buy those excuses. So many of us have

00:55:28.993 --> 00:55:34.287
developed a more socially acceptable excuse: ‘appropriate level of craft.’ We

00:55:34.287 --> 00:55:39.372
choose materials and processes, and subjects, and scales, and markets that

00:55:39.372 --> 00:55:44.248
will accept a level of labor and attention to detail that is lower than

00:55:44.248 --> 00:55:49.821
what we are capable of—as individuals or groups. This is a smart strategy—outside

00:55:49.821 --> 00:55:55.185
of the military and the space programs there is an infinitesimally small group

00:55:55.185 --> 00:56:00.409
of people who are willing to pay for something with error rates that have to

00:56:00.409 --> 00:56:05.842
be measured in a hundred thousandths of a unit. We can’t all be Apple. But it’s

00:56:05.842 --> 00:56:11.067
also a conceit that can justify the ugly which ought to have been beautiful,

00:56:11.067 --> 00:56:15.525
dragging intent into the junkyards of reconceptualized accidents.

00:56:15.525 --> 00:56:20.308
Do any of these ideas translate to things that do not fold-in earthly or

00:56:20.308 --> 00:56:25.698
intellectual sources, but which are not representative of anything? We could have

00:56:25.698 --> 00:56:30.347
a crucifix with a Jesus that is highly representative; we could have a

00:56:30.347 --> 00:56:35.198
relatively abstract cross; we could further abstract the Christ idea with

00:56:35.198 --> 00:56:40.453
some kind of incredibly simple dove, or fish, or cup. But let’s switch that out

00:56:40.453 --> 00:56:45.843
with a perfectly made unobtanium orb that a well-fed human made without coercion,

00:56:45.843 --> 00:56:50.896
with no title or statement or measurable impact. It’s not from nature, it is

00:56:50.896 --> 00:56:56.286
somehow magically perfect and human made. It has only two sources: the sphere and

00:56:56.286 --> 00:57:01.676
the material. Since the material doesn’t exist, there is no chance trying to beat

00:57:01.676 --> 00:57:06.729
up on it. But the sphere—the perfect, efficient shape; is it really perfect?

00:57:06.729 --> 00:57:11.984
While there are globes in nature, none are perfectly spherical—it’s as much the

00:57:11.984 --> 00:57:16.969
invention of the geometer as something fundamental in the universe. If this

00:57:16.969 --> 00:57:21.955
artsphere is beyond reproach as a physical thing, shouldn’t we need to know

00:57:21.955 --> 00:57:26.940
why it is here? Needn’t we come to conclusions about whether or not we want

00:57:26.940 --> 00:57:32.128
it? Would any of these facets contribute to whether or not we could measure an

00:57:32.128 --> 00:57:37.114
error that isn’t physical but conceptual? Although framed in a hypothetical

00:57:37.114 --> 00:57:42.302
physical form and world, this is fairly obviously an idea-based problem—and as

00:57:42.302 --> 00:57:47.691
such is quantum; the more we pin down the position on one of these variables, the

00:57:47.691 --> 00:57:52.475
faster the idea (which is still in someone’s mind) can morph in order to

00:57:52.475 --> 00:57:57.124
keep from being known for certain. This is a bit of an endorsement for

00:57:57.124 --> 00:58:02.446
conceptually-based artwork, and for story problems in general. If the particular

00:58:02.446 --> 00:58:07.230
manifestation isn’t the thing—but the idea is—it has the potential to be

00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:09.116
perfected. Until it is made.

00:58:09.116 --> 00:58:14.273
Nietzsche’s idea that artists glorify error is perfectly reasonable;

00:58:14.273 --> 00:58:19.891
correctness is the province of the math proof. Even within science, minute

00:58:19.891 --> 00:58:25.664
changes in scale and context will often yield unpredictable results, and the

00:58:25.664 --> 00:58:31.205
complexity of large scale changes—which is what extrapolating from models

00:58:31.205 --> 00:58:33.822
demands—if often wildly spasmodic.

00:58:33.822 --> 00:58:39.211
The arts, including design, music, dance, film, theater, and literature, are

00:58:39.211 --> 00:58:45.104
metaphysical industries. We describe what there is, and what it is like, and why we

00:58:45.104 --> 00:58:50.493
do practice a priori. And, sometimes, what it is is what it isn’t—as looking

00:58:50.493 --> 00:58:55.811
into deep space with only optical telescopes before the radio, we can’t see

00:58:55.811 --> 00:59:01.488
what can’t be seen—but it’s still there; and we can tell from the effects—we can

00:59:01.488 --> 00:59:06.662
plot a position. Tracking tight to the valid, accurate, and precise is an

00:59:06.662 --> 00:59:11.692
aesthetic choice—a strategy or affectation—not a binding charge. We are

00:59:11.692 --> 00:59:17.369
sometimes aided by the verifiable; but our work can be measured using only local

00:59:17.369 --> 00:59:22.758
and operational measurements. We have our own nomenclature, our own critical

00:59:22.758 --> 00:59:28.435
credentials, and our own infinitesimal calculus of enumerations. And we have the

00:59:28.435 --> 00:59:34.040
fuzzy effects we can measure at great distances if we cross-link our activities

00:59:34.040 --> 00:59:39.070
with things we can get a caliper on. Our laws need not exclude middles.

00:59:39.070 --> 00:59:43.783
The nature of synthesis is to call for the breakdown of things into their

00:59:43.783 --> 00:59:48.759
constituent parts; to take broken bits and recompile them into new forms with

00:59:48.759 --> 00:59:53.799
novel methods. The nature of control is limitation—no matter how much we think

00:59:53.799 --> 00:59:58.971
and work it through, we will never have custody of the whole of our creation. If

00:59:58.971 --> 01:00:04.208
we don’t allow for error as a function of the basic process, then we may also not

01:00:04.208 --> 01:00:09.052
be able to count on the occasionally transcendent breakthroughs that we are

01:00:09.052 --> 01:00:13.111
capable of making—we glorify not only the errant, but the true.

01:00:13.111 --> 01:00:15.805
Without error there may be no genesis.

01:00:15.805 --> 01:00:21.899
“Why is nature so nearly symmetrical? No one has any idea why. The only thing we

01:00:21.899 --> 01:00:26.778
might suggest is something like this: There is a gate in Japan, a gate in

01:00:26.778 --> 01:00:32.064
Neiko, which is sometimes called by the Japanese the most beautiful gate in all

01:00:32.064 --> 01:00:37.283
Japan; it was built in a time when there was great influence from Chinese art.

01:00:37.283 --> 01:00:42.772
This gate is very elaborate, with lots of gables and beautiful carving and lots of

01:00:42.772 --> 01:00:47.855
columns and dragon heads and princes carved into the pillars, and so on. But

01:00:47.855 --> 01:00:53.005
when one looks closely he sees that in the elaborate and complex design along

01:00:53.005 --> 01:00:58.020
one of the pillars, one of the small design elements is carved upside down;

01:00:58.020 --> 01:01:03.035
otherwise the thing is completely symmetrical. If one asks why this is, the

01:01:03.035 --> 01:01:08.322
story is that it was carved upside down so that the gods will not be jealous of

01:01:08.322 --> 01:01:13.675
the perfection of man. So they purposely put an error in there, so that the gods

01:01:13.675 --> 01:01:17.335
would not be jealous and get angry with human beings.”.

01:01:17.335 --> 01:01:26.159
Footnote 6. Richard Feynman, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I, page 52.

01:01:26.159 --> 01:01:29.178
Polished fat floats on dreams….

01:01:29.178 --> 01:01:34.963
Smothered in folds, standing on end; fouled theory gagging on the spray of its

01:01:34.963 --> 01:01:40.522
champions. Every shiny surface reflects an untamed lust for finger, breath,

01:01:40.522 --> 01:01:45.630
crease, lip, bottle. Wading into Mcluhan’s tetrad; cupping an overfed

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:49.612
breast; listening for a shameful history to melt away.

01:01:49.612 --> 01:01:55.674
Each node probes, fights, is overcome, divides, and is diluted. Staring at the

01:01:55.674 --> 01:02:01.499
first meme through wet lenses; purified in a flow, a mist, a squirt, a drop

01:02:01.499 --> 01:02:07.719
(crowned in reflection.) At a distance, shape seems a smooth and stable boundary

01:02:07.719 --> 01:02:10.789
(holding color and texture in repose.).

01:02:10.789 --> 01:02:18.176
Each body suckles, seeds, laughs, fears, bleeds, is worshiped, corrupted, and

01:02:18.176 --> 01:02:19.148
forgotten.

01:02:19.148 --> 01:02:25.093
Plunging in, matter is orifice—flesh, a soft and staining buttress for a limp

01:02:25.093 --> 01:02:30.647
craftsman. I am reading, forming, twisting, smashing, and losing hold of

01:02:30.647 --> 01:02:36.279
common, unconscious forms; finding that the order I’ve been nearing is an

01:02:36.279 --> 01:02:38.235
unshapable swarm of lust.

01:02:38.235 --> 01:02:43.487
You are what you see when you squint. The silhouette, the distance, the blur; each

01:02:43.487 --> 01:02:48.349
constraint defines your discrimination and demonstrates your skill at making

01:02:48.349 --> 01:02:51.656
sense of a world of misleading textures and details.

01:02:51.656 --> 01:02:57.343
You are what you can name. In order to transmit the immovable: we encode sound

01:02:57.343 --> 01:03:02.957
and mark phenomena in ways that stand in for matter. As each descriptive word

01:03:02.957 --> 01:03:08.792
advances, your options narrow and your character is disappointingly illuminated.

01:03:08.792 --> 01:03:13.947
You are what you can abstract. Never start with the surface; but with the

01:03:13.947 --> 01:03:19.817
essence. Select from the menu those tools which help you outline, to give shape, to

01:03:19.817 --> 01:03:22.538
float the most skin on the least fire.

01:03:22.538 --> 01:03:27.113
Pasts are piles of blocks that can be reassembled as needed—but that

01:03:27.113 --> 01:03:32.099
architecture could never exist in a gravitational universe. There are kids

01:03:32.099 --> 01:03:37.220
who are able to pronounce every word, varying pitch to indicate punctuation,

01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:42.547
and even string together sentences in ways that sound meaningful—but they can’t

01:03:42.547 --> 01:03:47.600
recall the thesis when quizzed. They dance dry in pouring rain. Focusing on

01:03:47.600 --> 01:03:52.517
phonetics gets us to the thunder before the lightning; camouflaging pubic

01:03:52.517 --> 01:03:57.571
pregnancies with a leafy collage of letters. We sprint to lines where names

01:03:57.571 --> 01:04:02.897
are silenced. On the other side, subjects are given voice—not that the page can

01:04:02.897 --> 01:04:08.360
hear them. When authors abandon words to the world, some are read exactly as they

01:04:08.360 --> 01:04:13.345
were written and then forgotten. Understanding is change; our words aren’t

01:04:13.345 --> 01:04:18.672
stored and understood in letters, but in chlorides. Our ideas aren’t garaged in

01:04:18.672 --> 01:04:23.794
paragraphs, but in neurons. Comprehension isn’t about mirror memory, but the

01:04:23.794 --> 01:04:28.984
ability to decipher, store, categorize and encrypt back into language in real

01:04:28.984 --> 01:04:33.901
time. Cognition can be measured in summaries, but it won’t be caged in by

01:04:33.901 --> 01:04:34.242
them.
